  <data name="en_pano43_description"><![CDATA[<div>In the northern part of Nigeria lies a piece of land of about 2,244 sq. km. It consists of a thick forest of beautiful flora and fauna, warm and cold springs, caves, wells and untamed wild life. This is Yankari Game Reserve. You are welcome. &nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>Yankari national Park provides a unique and beautiful environment for holiday seekers who come from far and near to see wildlife in their natural habitat, undisturbed by excessive human activities. Many facilities abound at Yankari for the pleasure of holiday makers. These include hotel accommodation, good restaurant and bars, the Wikki warm Spring, game viewing, tours around a variety of tourist attractions as well as weekend holiday programs.</div>]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano45_description"><![CDATA[The Wikki Warm Spring offers an exquisite bathing facility to tourists because the water is pure, crystal clear, and with a constant temperature of 31.10oC. It is situated in a gorge immediately below Wikki camp in Yankari. The water is 1.9m deep and 13m wide. The quantity of water that flows into the Park’s river system is approximately 100 million per day. The water gushes out from a string of chambers under the sand-stone cliff. Till date the actual source of the water is unknown.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano47_description"><![CDATA[<div>The spring gushes out from under a limestone rock surrounded by vegetation that boosts the ecosystem of the spring. The warm crystal clear water, which is free of reptiles and fish, offers an excellent facility for swimming and canoeing.</div><div><br></div><div>The area around the warm spring is believed to have served as a settlement of a community many centuries ago, because of some discoveries of debris from broken pots found around and inside the water.</div>]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano49_description"><![CDATA[The warm spring is located below a cliff. To get to the foot of this cliff one has to go down through series of steps with several landing. Once at the spring one quickly forgets the long descent down the steps and appreciates the work of mother nature as the tourist is presented with beautiful crystal clear warm pool from the Wikki spring.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano51_description"><![CDATA[This is the entrance to the wikki warm spring, it is made of red clay. This gives every visitor the real African feeling of red clay huts, thatched roof, and African art.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano53_description"><![CDATA[The Yankari National Park has many facilities to complement and enhance visitors experience in the park, inaddition to the park’s tourists resources. There is a reception complex, which houses a conference hall with capacity for 300 people. The hall has its own coffee room and toilet facilities, as well as souvenir and provision shops]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano55_description"><![CDATA[There is also a bar as well as discotheque, portable water, electricity and satellite television sets.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano57_description"><![CDATA[The services of the park is supported with standard restaurants which serve both continental and African dish for delight. Similarly, visitor accommodation is provided but in traditional style, modestly furnished and air conditioned chalets.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano59_description"><![CDATA[The corporate apartment at Yankari is well furnished with state of the art facilities, on the wall are sculptures of animals that gives visitors the aura of great safari feeling.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano61_description"><![CDATA[The corporate apartment at Yankari is well furnished with state of the art facilities, on the wall are sculptures of animals that gives visitors the aura of great safari feeling.]]></data>






  <data name="en_pano65_description"><![CDATA[The chalets consist of different accommodation units and categories that are tastefully furnished. Apart from the 110 furnished chalets, there is also student aim low-income hostel accommodation.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano67_description"><![CDATA[The Part also has an Activity Centre that meets the entertainment and night out needs of visitors.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano75_description"><![CDATA[Sporting facilities at the park includes: lawn tennis, badminton, squash, table tennis, dart and card games.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano73_description"><![CDATA[The jewel of all the opportunities is that visitors are free to undertake game viewing deep into the park’s forest in the parks trucks or in private vehicles. Visitors are guided around the safaris by highly trained and experienced guards. So grab your hiking boots, pick up a pair of binoculars and come experience this unspoiled part of Africa.]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano69_description"><![CDATA[<div>For the inquisitive tourist, the MUSEUM OF WILDLIFE TROPHIES at Yankari provides the answer to most of the historical, archaeological and wildlife you may need. The Museum is equipped with more than one thousand samples of animals and birds found in the Park, as well as literatures on their history and that of early staff of the Park. On entering the Museum, visitors are taken round the various sections by the most experienced staff of the Park who also serves as the curator.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>The museum is stocked with full mounts of wildlife such as leopards, crocodiles, etc. It also contains parts such as skins, tusks, horns and bones of wildlife species found in the Park. Hunting gears, traps and weapons retrieved from poachers are also displayed in the museum.</div>]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano71_description"><![CDATA[<div>The Dukkey Well at Yankari is made of 132 wells, this site was named after the famous Dukkey slave transit camp established during the last 19th century slave trade. The site was first used as a resting place for slaves and their traders, when travelling from Duguri to Pali during the slave trade period.</div><div>Some of them are interconnected, dug in the red sandstone area 31Km northwest of Wikki Camp along Mainamaji road. Slaves were said to have been used to dig these wells that served as water storage facilities for the traders and their slaves.</div>]]></data>



  <data name="en_pano77_description"><![CDATA[The Spring has relaxation area consisting of canopys and open space. At this point visitors can take a time off after the excitement of a warm swim. This area is ideal for sun bath, and refreshment as there is a light refreshment shop.]]></data>


